ue attacks and weapon  combinations as you'd expect from a  Souls game there's heavy emphasis on  progressing with your shield up at all  times  the developers stated that the first  part of the game will consist of  stressful tense gameplay where there are  less enemies but each encounter is  significant the latter part of the  gameplay will be heavily action-oriented  with more constant encounters this is a  good way to introduce new players to the  game while retaining the same tense  gameplay that the souls games are  for I expect traps to be a huge part of  the suspense in the first part of the  game and you can probably say that  coming through here here again we see  three unique attack animations for  simple our one spam one thing you'll  notice as we progress is the change in  atmosphere compared to Dark Souls there  are more similarities to Demon Souls  here and one of the reasons for this is  probably the darker environments  assumedly you can choose to progress  through these environments like you  would in the tomb of the giants  alternatively you can sacrifice your  left hand slot for a torch which  illuminates your environment so you can  see what's going to kill you  so many people have pointed out that the  torch blocks 100% physical damage here  which I assume will be changed since the  UI wrongly shows that you actually have  a a shield equipped at this point you  will have noticed that the character's  taking a fare beating firstly this is a  demo and it's common for Souls demos at  least to feature a over geared and over  leveled character this looks like an  early part of the game and the character  already has a huge supply of health  insurance and also equipment don't worry  too much about the nuances of gameplay  since it will actually take an entire  playthrough not just a demo to  accurately judge it in terms of rolling  it's worth noting that the player rolls  twice perpendicular to the original  position here instead of at the second  roll rotating around the enemies like it  would normally be expected in Dark Souls  developers have said that they've put  heavy emphasis on the context  appropriate reactions of enemies you're  behind this enemy and he reacts  appropriately they say this is part of  forging an experience that forces you to  strategize and think logically expect  every enemy or at least most of them to  have vastly improved AI and reactions  here the player also panics spamming his  d-pad he unequipped the torch and also  scrolls through his spell slot which  reveals a few things to us it has the  text l 2 semi-colon 10 assumedly this  means that the spell is at level 2 with  10 charges so this would be a new system  of upgrading spells but an old system of  spell charges from Dark Souls unless  it's just a part of the debug mode he  also a spam changes his item from drop  stone of healing two drops fluid of  healing to glisten whatever that means  later in the gameplay is revealed that  the stones of healing are  of consumable divine blessings but estes  remains in the game however the role s  displays is unknown at this point and if  you look really closely you can see  small red bars under the equipped  weapons assumedly this is an easier way  to check your durability this next part  was one of my favorites not only does  the character pull out a gigantic sword  reminiscent of something from berserk he  actually interacts with projectiles this  dwarf thing through his axis at the  player and since the player can't dodge  he whacks the first one away with an  attack from his sword this could add a  new element of depth to encounters and  it's another example of the game being  more cinematic while still retaining a  focus on the gameplay it's also  interesting to note that here the player  character switches through three weapon  slots instead of two whether this was  just a showcasing feature for the demo  will remain to be seen look at the  lighting in this scene I'll slow it down  the light seamlessly interacts with the  character and I can't get over how  impressive that is this scene shows a  unique atmosphere I mentioned before  it's more reminiscent of demon souls and  that seems to be the case I always found  demon souls to be more creepier and this  blood-stained hallway full of paintings  and torture instruments is doing a  pretty good job of that also the castle  design seems a bit similar to boletaria  in Demon Souls now this next optional  encounter has been encouraged by the  devs as well as a change in the level  design based around it so now it seems  like when you encounter a major or minor  boss in the level is subject to change  and perhaps the difficulty of the enemy  is going to be relative to the  environment you actually decide to  encounter it in ten amira states he  wants to break that traditional element  of having a boss wait to be killed at  the end of the level expect level design  to change to suit this and I expect  bosses to come and hunt you down just  like the silver chariot Dark Souls 2 is  looking a lot more hostile another big  thing in this fight scene is how the  ranged combat has changed it looks so  much better now  you now have limited movement while  attacking and each arrow actually takes  a portion of your stamina meaning  movement and stamina manage  we'll be a much larger part of ranged  combat this is a great addition since  ranged combat in previous games has felt  like it was only situationally viable  now the bow seems to be a viable method  of taking on enemies in general also as  a side note notice the attention to  detail here as a painting falls down  when it's disturbed in this part the  player two hands his weapon and gets hit  with a glancing blow from the Cyclops  but he takes only a bit of damage it  seems like where you get hit could play  a part and you'll notice the sound that  hit makes almost sounds like it's  hitting the shield on the players back  something cool would be backstab  immunity when a shield is on your back  or even just the shield on the back  playing a part in the combat there's a  few things to note on the bridge say the  camera jerks around to disorient the  player when falling and also the falling  animation has been drastically improved  also this is more law-related but note  that he calls them wyverns not dragons  the last section of footage seems to  show that there's still a POI system in  the game since the character tanks 2  hits before being launched into the air  on the third other player interactions  were also mentioned in the interview and  we can expect no major changes here only  improvements 10 Amira promises more  interactions with the players and better  interactions through the server based  systems finally I'll close with one part  of the gameplay I can't answer 100%  where there should be a humanity counter  there's this strange symbol that sort of  looks like a moon or a Sun or an eclipse  what does this mean let me know what you  think I have one theory but it's just as  likely to be correct as yours  we know that weather or perhaps time of  day will play a bigger part in the game  there are also many references to this  one rope bridge  in the first trailer the bridge was  depicted in a cold snowy dark  environment in the gameplay it was a  sunny warm environment with Dragons all  around in the interview the developers  mentioned that we've seen this bridge  before so I assume it's the same one we  also know that time travel plays a part  in the game  perhaps the Eclipse symbol symbolizes  the state of the world perhaps you have  to go to a different world state to  cross the bridge however this is just  speculation that I read on Twitter  courtesy of investigative live  journalism it might be better suited to  my analysis of the trailer from a lore  point of view and speaking of which  that's the next video I'll upload I am  to have my lower analysis complete in  the coming week and I plan to use  